From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: zibao <zibaone@proton.me>
Date: Mon, 26 Oct 2026 19:00:00 +0100
Subject: drm: bridge: dw-hdmi-cec: abort in-flight TX before gating the clock

The CEC block lives behind CECCLK (HDMI_MC_CLKDIS bit 5).  adap_disable()
masked the interrupts and gated that clock without ever aborting a frame
still in flight, and adap_enable() then wrote CEC_CTRL/CEC_STAT/CEC_LOCK
*before* ungating the clock.  A frame interrupted by a disable stays
latched in the controller: the CEC line is held low, every later transfer
completes with CEC_STAT_ERROR_INIT (tx_status=0x0030 in cec-adap), and the
adapter can never claim a logical address again.

Measured on an Orange Pi Zero 3 (H618, kernel 7.0.14) with a burst of
plug/plugout/plug events on the HDMI input: the physical address came
back, the logical address never did.  Three polls per address failed with
tx_status=0x0030, cec-adap gave up, and CEC stayed dead until the module
was reloaded (or the board rebooted).  Between two bursts the adapter
logged 36 and 92 transmit errors respectively.

Abort the frame before gating the clock, clear the status latch, and move
the enable-path register writes after the clock is ungated.  dw-hdmi-qp
already writes its CEC registers after clearing CEC_SWDISABLE.

Signed-off-by: zibao <zibaone@proton.me>
Link: https://github.com/zibaone/nixbian
---
--- a/drivers/gpu/drm/bridge/synopsys/dw-hdmi-cec.c
+++ b/drivers/gpu/drm/bridge/synopsys/dw-hdmi-cec.c
@@ -196,6 +196,19 @@
 	struct dw_hdmi_cec *cec = cec_get_drvdata(adap);
 
 	if (!enable) {
+		/*
+		 * Abort any transmission still in flight before gating the CEC
+		 * clock.  Gating the clock in the middle of a frame leaves the
+		 * controller driving the CEC line low: every later transfer then
+		 * completes with CEC_STAT_ERROR_INIT, the adapter never claims
+		 * its logical address again and CEC stays dead until the next
+		 * boot.  Observed after a HPD burst (plug/plugout/plug within
+		 * 1.2 s) while a logical address poll was in flight.
+		 */
+		dw_hdmi_write(cec, 0, HDMI_CEC_CTRL);
+		dw_hdmi_write(cec, ~0, HDMI_IH_CEC_STAT0);
+		cec->tx_done = false;
+
 		dw_hdmi_write(cec, ~0, HDMI_CEC_MASK);
 		dw_hdmi_write(cec, ~0, HDMI_IH_MUTE_CEC_STAT0);
 		dw_hdmi_write(cec, 0, HDMI_CEC_POLARITY);
@@ -204,14 +217,19 @@
 	} else {
 		unsigned int irqs;
 
+		/*
+		 * Ungate the CEC clock first: CEC_CTRL/CEC_STAT/CEC_LOCK are
+		 * part of the CEC block and cannot be relied on while CECCLK
+		 * is disabled.  dw-hdmi-qp follows the same order.
+		 */
+		cec->ops->enable(cec->hdmi);
+
 		dw_hdmi_write(cec, 0, HDMI_CEC_CTRL);
 		dw_hdmi_write(cec, ~0, HDMI_IH_CEC_STAT0);
 		dw_hdmi_write(cec, 0, HDMI_CEC_LOCK);
 
 		dw_hdmi_cec_log_addr(cec->adap, CEC_LOG_ADDR_INVALID);
 
-		cec->ops->enable(cec->hdmi);
-
 		irqs = CEC_STAT_ERROR_INIT | CEC_STAT_NACK | CEC_STAT_EOM |
 		       CEC_STAT_ARBLOST | CEC_STAT_DONE;
 		dw_hdmi_write(cec, irqs, HDMI_CEC_POLARITY);
